Disaster Evacuation Planner

A React + TypeScript + Vite DAA prototype for explainable emergency evacuation planning. It uses real latitude/longitude data for Hyderabad, Puri/Bhubaneswar, and Chennai scenarios, and the map base is the live Esri World Imagery satellite layer.

Run locally

Bash



pnpm install
pnpm dev



Open http://localhost:3000.

What is implemented

•
Hand-built binary min-heap priority queue in src/algorithms.ts.

•
Dijkstra with risk/congestion weighted roads, blocked-edge handling, predecessor reconstruction, and step trace capture.

•
BFS for minimum-hop comparison, reachability, and replay levels.

•
Greedy shelter assignment with capacity reduction, split evacuation, stranded reasons, and explicit priority-score breakdown.

•
Real Esri World Imagery tiles with Esri attribution and optional World_Boundaries_and_Places reference labels.

•
Dark/light control-room UI, phone bottom-sheet layout, language dropdown for 12 supported languages, Urdu RTL, localStorage preferences, export and print actions.

•
Built-in validation for blocked-edge avoidance, shelter capacity, and population accounting.

Suggested viva demo

1.
Start in the Hyderabad Musi scenario and point out the four danger zones, shelters, and ochre routes over the real satellite basemap.

2.
Click a zone and explain the weighted priority-score bar and the shelter WHY statement.

3.
Click a road on the map to block it, then press Re-plan. Explain how the assignment and stranded count change.

4.
Open the algorithm comparison section and contrast BFS minimum hops with Dijkstra risk-weighted cost.

5.
Press PLAY in Algorithm replay. Show the current node, tentative-cost table, relaxed edges, and live heap queue.

6.
Switch to Odisha or Chennai to demonstrate the same algorithms on a different real-place graph.

7.
Adjust priority sliders or shelter capacity and show an immediate recomputation and validation badge.

Data note

This is an educational DAA prototype using realistic static scenario data. It is not a source of live emergency instructions or official evacuation guidance.

